1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS

AC-2026-AI-INFRA-MEMORY-CREDENTIAL =E2=80=94 ACTIVE

Canonical fingerprint: internet-facing AI/MCP service -> vulnerability
exploitation -> host execution -> AI-runtime-aware inspection ->
in-memory proxy/master credential -> backend enumeration -> downstream
authority UNKNOWN.

Wiz=E2=80=99s August 2026 AI-infrastructure honeypot telemetry documented
attackers adapting post-exploitation to AI runtimes and retrieving
credentials from live process/module memory rather than ordinary
credential files. Observed activity included querying running LiteLLM
state for its master key and enumerating framework-specific
configuration and accessible backend models.

Stages: internet-facing AI/MCP service [CONFIRMED/T1] -> vulnerability
exploitation [CONFIRMED/T1] -> host execution [CONFIRMED/T1] ->
inspect AI runtime internals [CONFIRMED/T1] -> retrieve proxy/master
credential from process memory [CONFIRMED/T1] -> enumerate accessible
model backends [CONFIRMED/T1] -> resulting downstream abuse [UNKNOWN]
-> connected cloud/MCP authority [UNKNOWN].

The same telemetry also observed exploitation of internet-facing MCP
infrastructure for RCE and blind prompt injection causing agents with
execution tools to produce OS-level effects; some successful
blind-injection sessions progressed to payload retrieval and
cryptominer execution.

2 MATERIAL UPDATES

No existing canonical chain crossed the material-change threshold in that r=
un.

3-8 SYNTHESIS

Recurring mechanism: authority concentration. Compromised CI inherits
release identity; poisoned packages inherit developer/cloud
credentials; repository configuration inherits agent execution
authority; persistent agent configuration inherits later OAuth/tool
authority; compromised AI infrastructure can inherit runtime-resident
control-plane credentials.

Largest unresolved edge: AI runtime/master credential -> connected
MCP/cloud/service authority -> consequential external action.

Detection: unusual child processes of AI servers; runtime
introspection of framework modules/configuration; unexpected model
enumeration; master/proxy credential access followed by backend
enumeration; abnormal AI-infrastructure egress.

Containment: narrow AI proxy IAM; separate downstream identities;
restrict egress; runtime monitoring; isolate MCP services within the
AI proxy blast radius.

Safe lab: disposable LiteLLM-like proxy containing a fake in-memory
master credential and dummy backend; use a deliberately vulnerable
test endpoint and stop after demonstrating access to a dummy service.

